<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arado Flugzeugwerke</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1925">1925</a>–<a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arado Flugzeugwerke was a German aircraft maker founded in 1925 at Warnemünde near Rostock. It built trainers and seaplanes and, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, the Arado Ar 234, the first operational jet bomber, before it was dissolved at the end of the war.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Arado began as the Warnemünde factory of the Flugzeugbau Friedrichshafen firm, which produced land-based military aircraft and seaplanes during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. With its parent company it ceased operations after the war, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> restricted German aviation. In 1921 Heinrich Lübbe bought the factory and began building aircraft again for export, with Walter Rethel, previously of Kondor and Fokker, as head designer. In 1925 the company joined the Arado Handelsgesellschaft, a trading firm founded by the industrialist Hugo Stinnes Jr. to cover illegal trade in military equipment.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>When the Nazi government came to power in 1933, Lübbe took control of the company; just before, Walter Blume, formerly of Albatros, had replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rethel">Rethel</a> as designer. Arado became a supplier to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luftwaffe">Luftwaffe</a> with the Arado Ar 66, which remained one of its standard trainers into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>, and with some of its first fighters, the Ar 65 and Ar 68. In 1936 the Reich Aviation Ministry insisted that Lübbe join the Nazi party as a show of loyalty. He refused, was arrested and was forced to sell the company to the state. It was renamed Arado Flugzeugwerke GmbH and placed under Erich Serno and Felix Wagenführ.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the Ar 96 became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luftwaffe">Luftwaffe</a>'s most used trainer, and the Ar 196 became standard equipment on all larger German warships as a reconnaissance seaplane. Most of Arado's other designs were passed over in favour of competitors' products. Arado was the primary subcontractor for the Heinkel He 177 and built the Focke-Wulf Fw 190 and its components under licence. Its Ar 234 was the first jet-powered bomber. From August 1944 until their liberation by the Soviet army in April 1945, 1,012 slave labourers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freiberg">Freiberg</a>, a sub-camp of the Flossenbürg concentration camp, worked at the Arado factory, mostly Polish Jewish women and girls sent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswiecim">Auschwitz</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Arado was liquidated and broken up in 1945. The Ar 234 came too late to affect the outcome of the war, but it was a sign of things to come. The Ar 96 trainer continued to be built in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> by Zlin after the war, as the C.2B.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Arado Flugzeugwerke” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
